{"repositoryId":1181000961,"availability":"available","full":"rkchellah/Anzen","name":"Anzen","description":"AI Chief of Staff that monitors GitHub, Gmail and Slack - acts on your behalf without ever holding your credentials","category":"Productivity","language":"TypeScript","stars":1,"forks":0,"created":"2026-03-13T16:34:48Z","updated":"2026-10-12T07:36:50Z","branch":"main","homepage":"https://anzen-eight.vercel.app","topics":["ai-agent","auth0","automation","hackathon","nextjs","openai","productivity","tool-calling","typescript","vercel"],"demo":"https://anzen-eight.vercel.app/","color":"#63362a","ink":"#f8c7a8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-19T02:49:00.379Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-19T02:49:00.379Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-19T02:49:00.379Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-19T02:49:00.379Z","stars":1,"forks":0}],"technologies":["Next.js"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["An AI agent that acts on your behalf — without ever touching your credentials.","Most AI agents that connect to your tools store your OAuth tokens somewhere: a database, an env file, a session. That means your GitHub token, your Gmail access, your Slack credentials — all sitting inside an app trusting not to leak them.","Anzen holds none of it. You connect GitHub, Gmail, and Slack through Auth0 Token Vault. The tokens live there, sealed. When the agent needs to make an API call, it requests a short-lived access token, uses it once, and discards it. Anzen never stores long-lived credentials. The short-lived token it receives exists only in memory for the duration of one request."],"features":["Access mode per provider (read-only or read & write)","Profile (full name and preferred name)","Audit log of write actions, including their full parameters (e.g. email body), capped at 200 entries per user"],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/rkchellah/Anzen/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"e5a0437986ae6d7ba65177c2e11ac3b92f592c020d4a3455cbf8882061d2a952","extractedAt":"2026-10-19T02:49:00.461Z